## 1.7 Current Limitations (What GIA Does NOT Do)

| Gap | Description | Business Impact |
|-----|-------------|-----------------|
| **No FIFO/LIFO** | Only Average, Specific Identification, Standard supported | Cannot support US LIFO for tax or IFRS preference for FIFO |
| **No GL Posting** | All GIA ledgers are shadow subledgers | One GL book must use standard D365 costing |
| **No Overhead Allocation** | Cannot allocate indirect costs (warehouse, admin) to inventory | No full absorption costing |
| **No Landed Cost Integration** | Freight, duties handled separately in Landed Cost module | Not unified cost view |
| **No Period-End Recalculation** | No inventory close process for GIA ledgers | Limited period-end adjustments |
| **No Manufacturing** | BOM costing, production variances not supported | Distribution-only focus |
| **Limited Batch Tracking** | Specific identification only at batch level | No FIFO within batches |

### The Fundamental Problem with GIA as an Add-On

| Add-On Limitation | Impact on Adoption |
|-------------------|--------------------|
| **Requires LCS installation** | Additional deployment complexity; IT involvement |
| **Power Platform dependency** | Adds infrastructure; may conflict with existing Dataverse usage |
| **External service processing** | Latency; separate monitoring; potential sync issues |
| **Shadow-only architecture** | Cannot be the "book of record"; always secondary to standard costing |
| **Separate from Cost Accounting** | Two modules that should be one; customers confused about which to use |
| **Specialist expertise required** | Few partners know GIA; implementation resources scarce |

**Conclusion:** GIA's concepts are sound, but its add-on architecture limits adoption. The solution is to **bring these capabilities natively into D365 Finance and SCM** and merge them with Cost Accounting.

