# 3. Comparison & Pain Point Analysis (GIA vs Cost Accounting vs Classic Costing)

## 3.1 Comparison Table

| Dimension | Classic SCM/Finance Costing | Global Inventory Accounting (GIA) | Cost Accounting Module |
|-----------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------|
| **Primary Scope** | Inventory valuation for GL posting | Multi-ledger inventory valuation (shadow) | Managerial cost analysis, overhead allocation |
| **Data Model** | Inventory transactions → GL | GIA Ledgers with Conventions | Cost Accounting Ledger with dimensions |
| **Cost Elements/Accounts** | GL Main Accounts via posting profiles | Cost Element Policy with mapping rules | Cost Element Dimension (Primary + Secondary) |
| **Cost Objects** | Item + Dimensions (Site, Warehouse, Batch) | Cost Object Policy (Product-Site or Product-Site-Warehouse) | Cost Object Dimension (imported from Financial Dimensions) |
| **Multi-Ledger Capability** | Single valuation per LE | ✅ Multiple GIA Ledgers per LE | Single Cost Accounting Ledger per scope (can have multiple) |
| **Multi-Standard Support** | Limited (one valuation method) | ✅ Different Conventions per ledger (Avg, Standard) | Not designed for GAAP/IFRS differences |
| **GL Posting** | ✅ Primary GL posting | ❌ Shadow only (no GL posting) | ❌ Off-GL analytical layer |
| **Overhead/Allocation** | Limited (costing sheet for manufacturing) | ❌ Not supported | ✅ Full allocation engine (behavior, distribution, allocation, rollup) |
| **Valuation Methods** | FIFO, LIFO, Avg, Standard, Specific ID | Avg, Standard, Specific ID (no FIFO/LIFO) | N/A (doesn't value inventory) |
| **Analytics** | Standard financial reports | Power BI via Entity Store/Dataverse | Cost Control Workspace, Power BI |
| **Security Model** | Standard D365 security | Standard D365 security | Custom access list hierarchy with RLS |
| **Configuration Complexity** | Low-Medium | Medium | High |
| **Typical Implementer** | Finance consultant | SCM/Finance specialist | Cost Accounting specialist (rare) |
| **Target User** | Finance team | Finance/Controller | Cost accountants, Controllers, Managers |

## 3.2 Overlaps and Duplicated Concepts

| Concept | Classic Costing | GIA | Cost Accounting | Duplication Level |
|---------|-----------------|-----|-----------------|-------------------|
| **Cost accounts/elements** | Main Accounts | Cost Element Policy | Cost Element Dimension | **HIGH** - Three parallel definitions |
| **Cost object granularity** | Item Model Group + Dimensions | Cost Object Policy | Cost Object Dimension | **HIGH** - Three approaches |
| **Ledger/book structure** | One GL per LE | Multiple GIA Ledgers per LE | Cost Accounting Ledger | **MEDIUM** - Different purposes |
| **Hierarchies** | Financial Dimension Hierarchies | N/A | Dimension Hierarchies | **MEDIUM** - Separate maintenance |
| **Allocation rules** | Costing Sheet (manufacturing) | N/A | Allocation Policies + Bases | **LOW** - Different scope |
| **Currency handling** | Ledger currency + reporting currency | Per-ledger currency | Per-ledger currency | **LOW** - Aligned |

## 3.3 Where GIA is Strong (Valuation Backbone)

| Strength | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **True multi-ledger design** | Purpose-built for N parallel inventory valuations |
| **Convention reuse** | Policy bundles shared across ledgers for consistency |
| **Event-sourced architecture** | Single operational event → N accounting outcomes (auditable) |
| **Currency flexibility** | Different currency per ledger with exchange rate control |
| **Cost element decomposition** | Break inventory value into components (material, freight, etc.) |
| **Perpetual valuation** | Transaction-by-transaction tracking (weighted average) |

## 3.4 Where Cost Accounting is Strong (Managerial Layer)

| Strength | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| **Overhead allocation engine** | Full cost distribution, allocation, rollup capabilities |
| **Statistical measures** | Non-monetary bases for allocation (FTE, square meters, kWh) |
| **Cost behavior classification** | Fixed/Variable/Semi-variable analysis |
| **Reciprocal allocation** | Mutual service department allocations |
| **Managerial reporting** | Cost Control Workspace with budget comparison |
| **Role-based access** | Granular security for cost object controllers |

## 3.5 Why Adoption is Low for Both

### GIA Adoption Barriers

| Barrier | Root Cause | Evidence |
|---------|------------|----------|
| **Shadow-only limitation** | One GL must use classic costing; GIA is "extra work" | Customers question value if GL is still primary |
| **Missing valuation methods** | No FIFO/LIFO limits applicability | US companies with LIFO requirements can't fully use GIA |
| **Specialist knowledge required** | Convention policies need deep understanding | Few partners have GIA expertise |
| **No overhead allocation** | Cannot achieve full product cost | Requires Cost Accounting or manual processes |
| **Add-in deployment** | Requires LCS installation and Power Platform integration | Additional IT complexity |

### Cost Accounting Adoption Barriers

| Barrier | Root Cause | Evidence |
|---------|------------|----------|
| **Perceived redundancy with GL** | "We already have financial dimensions and Management Reporter" | Customers don't see incremental value |
| **Configuration burden** | 15+ entity types to configure before value | Long time-to-value discourages adoption |
| **Expertise scarcity** | Very few consultants know Cost Accounting well | Projects struggle to find resources |
| **Off-GL perception** | "If it doesn't post to GL, is it real?" | Finance teams skeptical of shadow analysis |
| **Manufacturing orientation** | Documentation and examples focus on manufacturing | Distribution companies don't see fit |
| **Hierarchy maintenance** | Hierarchies must be kept in sync with organization changes | Ongoing burden discourages adoption |

### The Fundamental Disconnect

```
CUSTOMER EXPECTATION:
"I want multi-ledger inventory valuation with overhead allocation,
 posting to GL, simple configuration, and easy adoption."

CURRENT REALITY:
┌─────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────┐
│ Classic Costing │    │      GIA        │    │ Cost Accounting │
│                 │    │                 │    │                 │
│ ✅ Posts to GL  │    │ ✅ Multi-ledger │    │ ✅ Overhead     │
│ ❌ Multi-ledger │    │ ❌ No GL post   │    │ ❌ No valuation │
│ ❌ Overhead     │    │ ❌ No overhead  │    │ ❌ Complex      │
└─────────────────┘    └─────────────────┘    └─────────────────┘
        │                      │                      │
        └──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┘
                              ▼
              "Which engine owns what?"
              "How do I connect these?"
              "Why is this so complicated?"
