# 4. Future-State Unified Multi-Ledger Costing Engine – Architecture & Design

## 4.1 Conceptual Architecture

### Fundamental Shift: Native D365 Capability, Not Add-On

> **The new Multi-Ledger Costing Engine is built INTO D365 Finance and Supply Chain Management as a native feature.** It merges the best concepts from GIA (multi-ledger valuation, conventions, cost elements) with Cost Accounting (overhead allocation, statistical measures, cost behavior) into a **single, unified costing capability**.

| Current State | Future State |
|---------------|---------------|
| GIA = Add-on requiring LCS + Power Platform | Multi-Ledger Costing = Native D365 feature |
| Cost Accounting = Separate analytical module | Allocation capabilities = Integrated into costing |
| Three engines (Classic + GIA + Cost Accounting) | One unified engine with multi-ledger support |
| Shadow subledgers only (GIA) | Full GL posting for any/all cost books |
| External async processing | Native D365 batch/real-time processing |

### Design Principles

| Principle | Description | Implication |
|-----------|-------------|-------------|
| **Native D365 integration** | Built into Finance and SCM, not external add-on | Standard deployment; no LCS add-in; no Power Platform dependency |
| **Merge GIA + Cost Accounting** | Single unified capability replacing two modules | One place to configure; one mental model |
| **Multi-book GL posting** | Any cost book can post to GL (or be shadow) | True multi-ledger accounting, not just shadow views |
| **Unified posting configuration** | One configuration interpreted per cost book | No triple-configuration burden |
| **Template-driven adoption** | Pre-built templates for 80% of scenarios | Reduce expertise requirements |
| **Progressive disclosure** | Simple by default, advanced when needed | Approachable for cost accountants |

